    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Births New Adventure Scooter Family, Three RW Models That Look More Like Motorcycles\nFord Maverick Hybrid Faces Recall Over 12V Battery Problem From 2023\n57 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nAn earlier Maverick Hybrid investigation into a potentially failing 12-volt battery connection focused on pickups that might refuse to start. Ford has revisited the problem, and on this occasion, the Dearborn-based automaker determined that a loss of power while driving may occur. In this event, drivers are left without power steering assistance, brake assistance, and exterior lighting.\nNo fewer than 8,630 examples of the 2022 model year Maverick Hybrid are being recalled, with Ford identifying the positive battery cable terminal as the problem area. Said terminal can loosen or detach from its cable, interrupting the 12-volt supply to the junction box. In case of such interruptions, the inability to shift into neutral also has to be highlighted.\nFord claims the joint between the terminal eyelet and the cable may corrode prematurely. Hydroxyacetic acid from dip soldering may corrode the ultrasonic weld that attaches the eyelet to the cable, deteriorating the solder in a few short years after the vehicle rolled off the assembly line. As you're well aware, Ford produces the Maverick for North America in Mexico at the factory where the Bronco Sport is also manufactured.\nIn the event of a 12-volt system issue, the vehicle may warn the driver in multiple ways. More specifically, the instrument cluster may display messages that include 'stop safely now, shift system fault, reduced power,' or 'powertrain malfunction.' These messages are joined by a red battery icon, a check engine light, a wrench, and the ABS warning light for good measure.\nSuspect Maverick Hybrids were assembled in the period between February 3, 2021, and May 12, 2022. Said vehicles haven't received the automaker's original remedy through customer satisfaction program 22B39 from June 2023, which covered 21,513 vehicles in the US market. Per 22B39 documentation, C1617E is the part number used for the subject connector.\nPhoto: Ford At the time, Ford engineers disconnected the 12-volt battery cable during vehicle-level testing, which indicated that a loss of motive power wasn't possible while driving. The safety boffins erroneously based that conclusion partly on the hybrid powertrain's DC-DC converter, which supplies the 12-volt electrical system while the Maverick Hybrid operates.\nGiven the aforementioned, customer satisfaction program 22B39 exclusively addressed the no-start condition associated with a bad or no 12-volt connection at all. The latest investigation kicked off in July 2026, after the Critical Concern Review Group reviewed vehicle owner questionnaires from the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration's database.\nSaid VOQs alleged that loose or broken connections may lead to a loss of motive power, increasing the risk of a crash at high speeds. The engineering department examined relevant field data, conducting further vehicle testing through October 2026 to assess whether propulsion may be interrupted while driving. Through September 1, 2026, the Dearborn-based manufacturer identified 427 warranty claims that relate to said concern.\n11 of those claims described power loss incidents, and FoMoCo is further aware of 11 vehicle owner questionnaires. The remedy? That comes in the form of a new 12-volt battery cable overlay between said battery and the junction box. The included replacement cable is designated NZ6T-14300-CA and boasts a different design from the original ultrasonically welded and dip-soldered connection.\nCustomers are scheduled to be notified by first-class mail in the period from November 23, 2026, through November 27, 2026. In the meantime, customers may determine if their 2022 Maverick Hybrids are being recalled by entering the 17-character VIN on the NHTSA's website.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2022 Ford Maverick Ford Maverick Ford recall Hybrid battery\nDownload: 2022 Ford Maverick Hybrid 12V battery cable recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1979 Chevy SS Monte Mino Mixes El Camino, Monte Carlo SS, and Corvette, It'll Be a Hot Wheels Car",
